    "id": "train-314",
    "question": "Berry wants to make sure he reads an average of 50 pages a day. This week he read 43 pages on Sunday, 65 pages on Monday, and 28 pages on Tuesday. He had a soccer game and fell asleep early on Wednesday so he read nothing. On Thursday he read 70 pages, and on Friday he read 56 pages. How many pages does he have to read on Saturday to reach his goal?",
    "gold": "88",
    "answer": "He has to read 350 pages a week because 7 x 50 = <<7*50=350>>350\nHe has read 262 pages because 43+65+28+70+56 = <<43+65+28+70+56=262>>262\nHe has to read 88 pages on Saturday because 350-262 =<<350-262=88>>88\n#### 88"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many pages Berry needs to read on Saturday to reach his goal of an average of 50 pages per day over the week, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total number of pages Berry needs to read over the week to achieve an average of 50 pages per day.\n2. Calculate the total number of pages Berry has read so far from Sunday to Friday.\n3. Determine the number of pages Berry needs to read on Saturday to reach his goal.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the total number of pages Berry needs to read over the week. Since there are 7 days in a week and he wants to read an average of 50 pages per day, the total number of pages he needs to read is:\n\\[ 50 \\times 7 = 350 \\text{ pages} \\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the total number of pages Berry has read from Sunday to Friday. He read 43 pages on Sunday, 65 pages on Monday, 28 pages on Tuesday, 0 pages on Wednesday, 70 pages on Thursday, and 56 pages on Friday. Adding these together, we get:\n\\[ 43 + 65 + 28 + 0 + 70 + 56 = 262 \\text{ pages} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find out how many pages Berry needs to read on Saturday to reach his goal of 350 pages. We do this by subtracting the number of pages he has already read from his total goal:\n\\[ 350 - 262 = 88 \\text{ pages} \\]\n\nTherefore, Berry needs to read \\(\\boxed{88}\\) pages on Saturday to reach his goal.",
